Suggests: vibeos-session,
Description: GNOME Shell extension of VibeOS, the shell for touch devices
 The shell@vibeos extension replaces GNOME Shell's top panel, overview and
 lock screen with a touch device's: Android-style gesture navigation
 (home, back, recents), the status bar, the notification shade with quick
 settings, heads-up notifications, the volume panel, the power menu, the
 lock screen with PIN or pattern, and the always-on display. The VibeOS launcher
 is started as the home screen.
 .
 The extension is for GNOME Shell 51. It is not turned on for the regular
 GNOME session; the vibeos-session package provides a "VibeOS"
 session that always runs it.

Package: vibeos-session
Architecture: all
# /etc/xdg/gnome-mimeapps.list was vibeos-installer's
Breaks: vibeos-installer (<< 1:0.1.3+audit3~)
Replaces: vibeos-installer (<< 1:0.1.3+audit3~)
Depends: gnome-session (>= 51~),
         gnome-shell-extension-vibeos (= ${source:Version}),
         ${misc:Depends},
Suggests: gdm3,
Description: VibeOS session
 A "VibeOS" session for display managers: GNOME's own session with GNOME
 Shell in the vibeos mode, which always runs the vibeos extension.
 .
 Also systemd units for a phone without a display manager (vibeos.service
 starts the session on tty7 as one user), and vibeos-dock, which switches to the
 regular GNOME desktop while an external display is connected. None of these
 units is enabled on installation; see README.Debian.
 .
 A udev rule keeps the phone's own storage (UFS or eMMC: Android's partitions
 and the system's) out of Files and the file picker; SD cards and USB drives
 still show.
 .
 The session's default apps: App Installer for .deb files, Files for folders.

Package: vibeos-daemons
Architecture: any
Section: admin
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends},
Description: VibeOS system helpers
 Small system programs used by the VibeOS shell and apps:
  * vibeos-auth checks the lock screen's PIN or pattern through PAM (the PIN or
    pattern is the user's own password);
  * vibeos-wakeup wakes the phone from suspend at the times apps ask for (alarm
    clocks, calendar reminders), over D-Bus (vibeos.Wakeup), and publishes
    the next alarm clock;
  * input-boost raises the GPU's (and optionally the CPUs') clock floor while
    the touchscreen is touched. It is not enabled by default: its floor must
    be tuned for the device;
  * vibeos-theme computes the system's colours from a seed colour or the
    wallpaper and stores them in vibeos.Theme (whose schema is here), which
    the apps follow, light or dark.

Package: vibeos-unifiedpush
Architecture: any
Section: net
Depends: glib-networking,
         ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends},
Description: push messages for the apps of VibeOS (UnifiedPush distributor)
 vibeos-unifiedpush brings apps their push messages, as Android's push
 service does: one connection to the push server (push.vibeos.top, an ntfy
 server) for all of them, and each message handed to its app over D-Bus,
 which starts the app if it does not run. Messages sent while the phone was
 offline come once it is back.
 .
 Apps register through the UnifiedPush D-Bus spec
 (org.unifiedpush.Distributor2), directly or with libvibeos's
 VibeosUnifiedPush, and their servers send Web Push messages (RFC 8030,
 encrypted by RFC 8291) to the endpoint each registration gets. It runs as a
 systemd user unit from login, and is D-Bus activatable.

Package: sim-apn
Architecture: all
Section: net
Depends: python3,
         python3-gi,
         gir1.2-nm-1.0,
         mobile-broadband-provider-info,
         ${misc:Depends},
Recommends: modemmanager,
            network-manager,
Description: mobile data profile for every new SIM
 sim-apn watches ModemManager for SIMs that no NetworkManager connection
 applies to, and adds one for each: named after the operator, its APN from
 mobile-broadband-provider-info, pinned to that SIM, with roaming off. A
 connection made by hand, and a SIM that already has one, are left alone.

Package: vibeos-launcher
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends},
Recommends: mesa-vulkan-drivers,
            libegl-mesa0,
Description: home screen of VibeOS, in Vulkan or OpenGL ES
 The VibeOS launcher is the home screen of VibeOS: pages with a dock, an app
 drawer that follows the finger, widgets, icon packs and icon shapes, and
 parallax wallpapers. It is a Wayland client drawn with Vulkan 1.0, or with
 OpenGL ES 3.0 where the GPU has no Vulkan driver, and scrolls with
 libspongy's Android physics.
 .
 It is started by the vibeos GNOME Shell extension, which makes its
 window the desktop.

Package: libspongy-1-0
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: same
Section: libs
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends},
Description: Android scroll physics (stretch overscroll, fling, springs)
 libspongy reimplements Android's scrolling physics in C: the stretch
 overscroll (EdgeEffect), the fling (OverScroller), velocity tracking,
 spring animations and the interpolators, to the numbers of Android's own.
 .
 This package contains the shared library.

