Package: vibeos-dictate
Architecture: any
Depends: gstreamer1.0-plugins-base,
         ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends},
Recommends: vibeos-dictate-en | vibeos-dictate-de | vibeos-dictate-es | vibeos-dictate-fr | vibeos-dictate-it | vibeos-dictate-ru,
            gstreamer1.0-pulseaudio,
Suggests: vibeos-dictate-de,
          vibeos-dictate-en,
          vibeos-dictate-es,
          vibeos-dictate-fr,
          vibeos-dictate-it,
          vibeos-dictate-ru,
Description: dictation for VibeOS
 Dictate turns speech into text on the phone itself: a microphone button and a
 document, where what is said appears as it is said, with nothing sent
 anywhere. The document is plain text, edited and copied as any other. It
 hears English, German, Spanish, French, Italian and Russian, the language
 chosen in its app bar.
 .
 Vibe, in the same package, is a spoken conversation: it listens, answers
 from a language model (VibeOS's server, or a GGUF file on the phone through
 llama.cpp) and speaks the answer with a Piper voice, downloaded with the
 language when it is chosen.
 .
 The recognisers are not part of VibeOS: vibeos-dictate loads Moonshine's
 (English, German, Spanish) and Vosk's (French, Italian, Russian) libraries
 from /usr/lib/vibeos-dictate and their models from
 /usr/share/vibeos-dictate/models. The packages vibeos-dictate-moonshine,
 vibeos-dictate-vosk and one per language (vibeos-dictate-en,
 vibeos-dictate-de, ...), made by tools/vibeos-dictate-speech in the source,
 install them.

Package: vibeos-installer
Architecture: any
Depends: packagekit,
         vibeos-sandbox (>= ${source:Version}),
         ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends},
Recommends: appstream,
Description: app installer for VibeOS
 App Installer installs .deb package files, and installs and uninstalls
 packages from the software sources, for the user and for other apps (such
 as Software) over D-Bus. Before anything changes it shows the package: the
 app's name and icon, its version, sizes and description, whether it is new,
 an upgrade, a downgrade or a reinstall (both versions side by side), and the
 other packages it installs or removes. It refuses to uninstall VibeOS
 itself, the shell and the system's essential packages.
 .
 PackageKit does the installing and polkit asks for the password. It is the
 VibeOS session's default app for .deb files.

Package: vibeos-software
Architecture: any
Depends: vibeos-installer (>= ${source:Version}),
         vibeos-archive-keyring,
         webp-pixbuf-loader,
         ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends},
Recommends: gnome-keyring,
Description: app store for VibeOS
 Software is the VibeOS store's app, on the catalog at vibeos.top: featured
 apps, apps and games by category, search with suggestions, and an app's page
 with its screenshots, description, what is new, the permissions it declares,
 its ratings and reviews and its details. Mine lists the apps installed, the
 updates (with Update all) and, with a store account, the apps the account
 had on other devices.
 .
 A store account, confirmed by a code sent by email, rates and reviews apps,
 marks reviews helpful and reports them. Its token is kept in the login
 keyring.
 .
 Installing, updating and uninstalling go through App Installer, and polkit
 asks for the password: Software never sees it.
 .
 A user timer looks for updates without the window, after login and every
 six hours, and suggests each new version once in a notification that opens
 Mine. Software's update setting (Wi-Fi only, any network or never) applies.

Package: vibeos-archive-keyring
Architecture: all
Multi-Arch: foreign
Section: misc
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
Description: VibeOS archive's signing key and apt source
 The OpenPGP key that signs VibeOS's apt archive at https://vibeos.top/apt
 (ed25519, 0802 5010 73FA FEAC CFF5  B808 FF24 1881 81F7 82CB), and the
 archive as an apt source (suite forky, component main), so that apt,
 PackageKit and Software get new versions of VibeOS and its apps.
 .
 The source is a configuration file: a change to it (or its removal) is kept
 through upgrades. The archive's opt-in mutter component is not in it.

Package: vibeos-sandbox
Architecture: any
Depends: bubblewrap (>= 0.8),
         xdg-dbus-proxy,
         xdg-desktop-portal (>= 1.18),
         ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends},
Recommends: wireplumber (>= 0.5.6),
            xdg-desktop-portal-gnome,
Description: sandbox for the apps installed from Software on VibeOS
 The apps installed from Software, or from a .deb file with App Installer, run
 in a sandbox (bubblewrap): the system read-only, a home of their own, no
 other app's files, no system bus, and only the parts of the session bus that
 their permissions allow, through xdg-dbus-proxy. The portals identify them
 as they identify Flatpak apps, and ask the user before an app gets the
 location or the camera.
 .
 Each app's permissions (network, location, camera, microphone, contacts,
 calendar, phone, SMS, files, running in the background) are kept in the
 desktop's permission store, and set in Settings, Apps. WirePlumber hides the
 microphones and cameras from an app without the permission.
 .
 VibeOS's own apps do not run sandboxed.

Package: vibeos-iconpack-gnome-free
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
Recommends: vibeos-launcher,
Description: gnome-free icon pack for the VibeOS launcher
 gnome-free (called Round before VibeOS): colourful icons for VibeOS's apps
 and the GNOME apps it keeps, a white Material Symbols glyph on a gradient
 disc in each app's colour. VibeOS Settings' Home screen switches packs.

Package: vibeos-iconpack-vibeos
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
Recommends: vibeos-launcher,
Description: VibeOS icon pack for the VibeOS launcher
 The VibeOS icons, the launcher's default: a large Material Symbols glyph on
 a squircle in each app's colour (white on colour, or coloured on white), for
 VibeOS's apps and the GNOME apps it keeps. Installing it makes it the
 launcher's icon pack; VibeOS Settings' Home screen switches packs.

Package: vibeos-parallax-solar-system
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
Recommends: vibeos-launcher,
Description: NASA solar-system parallax wallpaper for the VibeOS launcher
 A layered wallpaper that moves with the device's tilt on the VibeOS
 launcher: the Sun, Earth, the Moon, Mars, Saturn and Jupiter from NASA's
 public-domain images over a drawn star field. Installing it makes it the
 default wallpaper; GNOME Settings' Background panel lists it too.

Package: vibeos-video
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends},
Recommends: gvfs-fuse,
Description: video player for VibeOS
 VibeOS Video Player plays videos with mpv (libmpv, drawn through its OpenGL
 renderer) under an Android player's controls: back and the title at the top,
 skip back, play/pause and skip forward in the middle, the time and a seek bar
 at the bottom. A tap shows or hides them, a double tap on either side skips
 10 seconds, and where a film was left is remembered. Files on a share play
 through GVfs's FUSE path, the share mounted on demand. It is the default
 application for videos.

Package: vibeos-terminal
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends},
Description: terminal for VibeOS
 VibeOS Terminal runs the user's shell in GNOME's terminal widget (VTE),
 made for a touchscreen: a tap brings the keyboard, which opens over the
 window rather than shrinking it, with a row of Esc, Tab, Ctrl, Alt and the
 arrow keys on it (Ctrl and Alt sticky, as Termux's); a long press selects a
 word with handles to widen it and a toolbar to copy or paste; a drag of one
 finger or two scrolls the history and flings it; and a pinch zooms the
 text. In less, man or vim scrolling sends the cursor keys, and to a program
 that takes the mouse, such as htop or mc, the mouse wheel, as a wheel would.
